    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/410": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - SOM Oncology Program",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States (3-309-1277) The University of Maryland is recruiting for a tenure-track physician, scientist or physician-scientist with expertise in real world data and novel clinical trial design/execution with particular emphasis on oncology sciences to serve in the role of Director of Cancer Research in the Center for Real World Data/Adaptive Clinical Trials at the University of Maryland-Institute for Health Computing (UM-IHC) and Assistant Director for Learning Health System and Pragmatic Oncology Research at the UM Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center (UMGCCC). (3-309-1277) The University of Maryland is recruiting for a tenure-track physician, scientist or physician-scientist with expertise in real world data and novel clinical trial design/execution with particular emphasis on oncology sciences to serve in the role of Director of Cancer Research in the Center for Real World Data/Adaptive Clinical Trials at the University of Maryland-Institute for Health Computing (UM-IHC) and Assistant Director for Learning Health System and Pragmatic Oncology Research at the UM Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center (UMGCCC). The UM-IHC is a large-scale initiative focusing on computational and analytical methods to build a learning health system for improving the quality of life and longevity of the citizens of Maryland. The UM-IHC, located in North Bethesda, MD, is a collaborative effort of the University of Maryland Baltimore, the University of Maryland College Park, and the University of Maryland Medical System (UMMS), funded with initial grants from the State of Maryland’s MPower initiative and Montgomery County, MD. A major focus of the Institute is to advance the utilization and clinical relevance of real-world clinical data, which is based on the supposition that little information is available at present to guide the management of patients outside the highly structured environment that defines traditional clinical trials. By decentralizing clinical research, participation in the clinical research enterprise among under-represented and underserved communities can be amplified, thereby generating data on health and human disease that is representative of the various communities at hand. The expected salary range for Assistant Professors is $300,797 (min) and $450,000 (max). The referenced salary range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. This salary range does not include all components of the departmental faculty compensation program or pay from participation in departmental variable compensation programs. Therefore, the actual compensation paid to the selected candidate may vary from the salary range stated herein. For more information, please contact the hiring department. Expected rank is Assistant Professor or higher, however, rank and tenure status is dependent on selected candidate’s qualifications. The referenced salary range represents the minimum and maximum salaries for this position and is based on the University of Maryland School of Medicine’s good faith belief at the time of posting. Not all candidates will be eligible for the upper end of the salary range. The actual compensation offered to the selected candidate may vary and will ultimately depend on multiple factors, which may include the successful candidate's geographic location, skills, work experience, internal equity, market conditions, education/training and other factors, as reasonably determined by the University. UMB offers a comprehensive benefits package that prioritizes wellness, work/life balance, and professional development. This position participates in a retirement program that must be selected and is effective on your date of hire. Faculty receive a generous leave package that includes over 4 weeks of vacation accrued each year, paid holidays, personal leave, unlimited accrual of sick time, and comprehensive health insurance; professional learning and development programs; tuition remission for employees and their dependents at any University System of Maryland school. You are also invited to view the Department of Medicine benefits for full-time faculty members. For immediate consideration, please send a cover letter and current CV, as well as contact information of three references. Though not required, candidates are also invited to include a perspective statement on equity, diversity, inclusion, and civility. For additional questions after application, please email facultypostings@som.umaryland.edu The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. Faculty applying should be experienced in clinical research with allied expertise in real world evidence, novel clinical trial design (such as adaptive and pragmatic studies), and a deep understanding of one or more clinical domains. They should have experience leading a research program with a record of accomplishment in securing independent extramural funding and publishing senior author manuscripts. Qualified candidates are researchers who are already exploring how data science can be merged with clinical trial methodologies in the context of a multi-site health system to facilitate large-scale trials. The successful candidate will be expected to supervise and mentor staff and fellows within the UM-IHC and may provide clinical service in their selected field. If the candidate will provide patient care through clinical responsibilities, a medical degree from a recognized accredited university (or foreign equivalent) is required and candidates must be BC/BE, and eligible for a license to practice medicine in the State of Maryland. The role of Director of Cancer Research in the Center for Real World Data/Adaptive Clinical Trials will emphasize three elements: A. Team building that includes: Existing UM-IHC members specializing in population health, community health, and clinical content Existing UM-IHC data analysts and database engineers with direct access to the UMMS database and unique expertise in artificial intelligence, machine learning, bioinformatics, and biostatistics Highly qualified external candidates that will fortify a multi-disciplinary team that includes nursing sciences, clinical trial statistical sciences, and other allied roles B. Infrastructure expansion: Lead a team-based strategic plan that leverages the UMMS clinical operations infrastructure to develop a nimble, dynamic, and high-capacity platform for real-world data capture and pragmatic clinical trial implementation in cancer research. Work with innovative computational scientists and clinicians to anticipate the next era of decentralized cancer research, and build a forward-thinking strategy that enables the Center to capitalize on pioneering innovation Contemplate and develop partnerships with FDA in the planning phase of cancer pragmatic trials to maximize the potential relevance of our results on governmental policy and/or clinical practice Expand existing and develop new partnerships between the University of Maryland and Community Leaders in West Baltimore and other regions of Maryland to maximize participation among community members. C. Completion of Adaptive/Pragmatic Clinical Research Oversee the successful completion of clinical trials in cancer research, programmed through the Electronic Health Record, which use novel designs and approaches that advance topics across the medical spectrum. Prioritize research opportunities that emerge from GCCC and from within the UM-IHC itself. Partner with community leaders in West Baltimore and other regions for disseminating research findings to community members Leverage emergent data and innovation to win extramural funding and new public-private partnerships involving UM-IHC, respectively. UMGCCC: The role of Assistant Director for Learning Health System and Pragmatic Oncology Research at UMGCCC includes the following responsibilities: Designated leader of the UMGCCC Program in Big Data and Real-World Evidence reporting to the Cancer Center Director through the supervising Associate Director for Quantitative Science Serves as the oncology Czar within IHC providing the key link between UMGCCC members and the IHC community and technology ecosystem Facilitate UMGCCC research projects through IHC by linking project team with the necessary IHC expertise and to identify data science and computational platforms required for successful execution of the projects Lead initiatives around the creation of a robust data management system for UMGCCC leading to a searchable and accessible Data Commons that combines available data through the UMGCCC Clinical Informatics Shared Resource with its basic, clinical and population research data to propel UMGCCC research and discovery to improve patient outcome in the catchment area Guide the development of a Real-World Evidence program at UMGCCC through the strategic acquisition and deployment of data (electronic health records information about SDH, clinical, lab, and imaging datasets), technology (computing, analytic and storage platforms) and expertise (UMGCCC faculty, UMSOM, UMMC, UMMS and partners across the USM campuses) Create collaboration with pharma and regulatory agencies that enable unique population of cancer patients treated at UMGCCC to guide therapeutic advances that address the needs of cancer patients from URM background relevant to our catchment area Expected faculty rank for these positions will be Assistant Professor or higher, however, final rank, tenure status and salary will be commensurate with selected candidate’s qualifications and experience. Qualified candidates will contribute to the research, teaching, and/or clinical missions of the University, sustain an externally funded individual research program consistent with the School of Medicine’s vision, and enhance research opportunities for other School of Medicine faculty and trainees. The appointee should also show potential for superior teaching, service, and research, scholarship, or creative performance, commensurate with the mission of the institution."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/328": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - SOM Peds Neonatology",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: MD, United States The Division of Neonatology is a rapidly growing, academically oriented division. We are currently seeking an Assistant Professor or Associate Professor–level faculty member to join our expanding team. This faculty position will be based at the University of Maryland Medical Center (UMMC) in downtown Baltimore and may include clinical duties at any of the Level III NICUs within our NICU network. The position will contribute to clinical care, education, and quality initiatives within the Level IV NICU at UMMC and across our NICU network. The successful candidate will hold a faculty appointment in the Department of Pediatrics, University of Maryland School of Medicine, at the rank of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor, commensurate with experience. University of Maryland Medical Center Position Highlights UMMS has >4,600 affiliated physicians who provide primary and specialty care in more than 150 locations and at 13 hospitals. Level IV- 52 State-of-the-Art single-family-room Neonatal Intensive Care Unit with an accredited Neonatal-Perinatal fellowship Program with three dedicated teams for rounding transport and delivery/transport and a growing POCUS program. 24/7 in-house Advanced Practice Providers Participation in multi-site UMMC Neonatal Quality Improvement Projects Member of Vermont-Oxford Network. Collaboration with multiple level I - III NICUs affiliated with the UMMS and Mercy Medical Center (28 SFR NICU) PICU providing ECMO and post-cardiac surgery care The Maryland Regional Neonatal Transport Program (MRNTP) services that provides referrals from more than 30 hospitals in the region. High risk NICU Follow Up Program Center for Advanced Fetal Care (CAFC) Collaboration with Mt. Washington Pediatric Hospital for continuity of care Research funding opportunities provided at the University of Maryland Institute for Clinical Translational Research (ICTR), UMSOM Pivot grants and other mural grants Research collaboration with the renowned Institute of Genome Sciences (IGS) Benefits/Perks: Generous leave package Dependent tuition remission for any school within the University of Maryland system Excellent pension/retirement plans Very competitive salary with bonus opportunity As the oldest public medical school in the United States, the University of Maryland School of Medicine has an established tradition of outstanding clinical care, education, and research. The Department of Pediatrics is deeply committed to promoting children’s health in the community and across the state, while supporting innovative clinical programs and expanding research initiatives. The University of Maryland Golisano Children’s Hospital, named in recognition of a historic $50 million-dollar philanthropic investment, is part of the Golisano Children’s Alliance, a national network of premier pediatric hospitals. This investment expands our clinical programs, pediatric research, and innovation. Our hospital benefits from enhanced collaboration, sharing clinical expertise, research insights, and educational opportunities as part of the Golisano Children’s Alliance to drive continuous improvement in pediatric care. We are located near the downtown Inner Harbor area, just one of Baltimore’s many outstanding attractions. From fine arts and orchestras to professional sports teams, first-class dining and shopping to a wealth of historical sites, our city offers a full range of recreational and cultural opportunities. Washington, D.C., Philadelphia and even New York City are within easy reach, as well as beaches to the east and mountains to the west. Educational excellence abounds, as do family-friendly neighborhoods and urban living options. Visit us online at www.ummsphysician.jobs to learn more. Refer to position # 03-314-556 The referenced salary range represents the minimum and maximum salaries for this position and is based on the University of Maryland School of Medicine’s good faith belief at the time of posting. Not all candidates will be eligible for the upper end of the salary range. The actual compensation offered to the selected candidate may vary and will ultimately depend on multiple factors, which may include the successful candidate's geographic location, skills, work experience, internal equity, market conditions, education/training and other factors, as reasonably determined by the University. Expected rank for this position will be Assistant Professor or higher, however, final rank and tenure status will be commensurate with selected candidate’s experience. Salary range: Assistant Professor $200,000 to $220,000 Associate Professor $230,000 to $260,000 UMB offers a comprehensive benefits package that prioritizes wellness, work/life balance, and professional development. This position participates in a retirement program that must be selected and is effective on your date of hire. Faculty receive a generous leave package that includes over 4 weeks of vacation accrued each year, paid holidays, personal leave, unlimited accrual of sick time, and comprehensive health insurance; professional learning and development programs; tuition remission for employees and their dependents at any University System of Maryland school. UMB is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, color, religion, national origin, disability, protected Veteran status, age, or any other characteristic protected by law or policy. If you need a reasonable accommodation for a disability for any part of the employment process, please submit an online request or contact leave_and_accom@umaryland.edu . Please note that only inquiries concerning a request for reasonable accommodation will be responded to from this Doctorate or recognized terminal degree in the field of specialization. Shown potential for superior teaching, service, and research, scholarship, or r creative performance, commensurate with the mission of the UMB. May require unrestricted license in the State of Maryland. The appointee should also show potential for superior teaching, service, and research, scholarship, or creative performance, commensurate with the mission of the institution. 24/7 in-house Advanced Practice Providers Participation in multi-site UMMC Neonatal Quality Improvement Projects Member of Vermont-Oxford Network. Collaboration with multiple level I - III NICUs affiliated with the UMMS and Mercy Medical Center (28 SFR NICU) PICU providing ECMO and post-cardiac surgery care The Maryland Regional Neonatal Transport Program (MRNTP) services that provides referrals from more than 30 hospitals in the region. High risk NICU Follow Up Program Center for Advanced Fetal Care (CAFC) Collaboration with Mt. Washington Pediatric Hospital for continuity of care Research funding opportunities provided at the University of Maryland Institute for Clinical Translational Research (ICTR), UMSOM Pivot grants and other mural grants Research collaboration with the renowned Institute of Genome Sciences (IGS)"
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/351": {
      "title": "Associate Professor - SOM Oncology Program",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: MD, United States UMGCCC and the School of Medicine are recruiting four tenure track Assistant to Associate Professors with expertise in various disciplines. The Marlene & Stewart Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center (UMGCCC) and the School of Medicine (UMSOM) are recruiting four tenure track Assistant to Associate Professors with expertise in the below disciplines. The successful candidate will be a member of the UMGCCC and will hold an appointment in an academic department in the UMSOM. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent and at least 4 years of relevant faculty-level experience. All applicants are encouraged, but preference will be given to individuals who have already been successful in obtaining significant research funding. The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. Applicants must have a PhD or equivalent and at least 4 years of relevant faculty experience. Expected Salary Range: $125,000 to $184,500. The referenced salary range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. This salary range does not include all components of faculty compensation program or pay from participation in variable compensation programs. Therefore, the actual compensation paid to the selected candidate may vary from the salary range stated herein. For more information, please contact the hiring department. The referenced salary range represents the minimum and maximum salaries for this position and is based on the University of Maryland School of Medicine’s good faith belief at the time of posting. Not all candidates will be eligible for the upper end of the salary range. The actual compensation offered to the selected candidate may vary and will ultimately depend on multiple factors, which may include the successful candidate's geographic location, skills, work experience, internal equity, market conditions, education/training and other factors, as reasonably determined by the University. UMB offers a comprehensive benefits package that prioritizes wellness, work/life balance, and professional development. This position offers a retirement program that must be selected and is effective on your date of hire. Faculty receive a generous leave package that includes over 4 weeks of vacation accrued each year, paid holidays, personal leave, unlimited accrual of sick time, and comprehensive health insurance; professional learning and development programs; tuition remission for employees and their dependents at any University System of Maryland school. Note: Leave and tuition benefits are prorated for part-time employees. Applications should submit an online application, referencing the appropriate position number . Qualified applicants should submit a single PDF file containing a short cover letter, a detailed curriculum vitae, contact information for three or more references, up to three reprints of representative publication(s), and a 3-to-5-page description of future research directions. Responses should reference one of the following position numbers: Position #03-303-162: Tumor Immunology & Immunoengineering Rigorously trained with high impact discovery in basic science including immunology and inflammation in cancer development and therapy, or in immunoengineering. ​ Priority will be given to funded early career tumor immunologists (MD/PhD or PhD) with focused research interests in solid tumor immunotherapy. Position #03-334-004: Cancer Therapeutics Focus is in identifying and studying molecular targets that inform the development of strategies and treatments to improve therapeutic outcomes in high-priority cancer areas, working, participating and collaborating with researchers in CT and the other programs in UMGCCC as necessary, as well as relevant clinicians within the CT program. Participating, informing and being informed by the Translational Working Group of the cancer center will be a relevant component of helping strengthen the CT program. ​ Position #03-334-005: Cancer Biology Cancer Biology aims to hire a researcher with research focus in any area of cancer biology, including cancer genomics and epigenetics, informatics, tumor initiation, evolution, microenvironment, and metastasis, intrinsic and acquired treatment resistance, and target identification with the potential to develop diagnostic markers and novel therapeutics. ​​ Areas of Interest ​ : Expertise in developing strategies to improve diagnosis, inform treatment, and evaluate treatment response. Position #03-307-180: Population Science The Population Science Program seeks to recruit a cancer epidemiologist with expertise in cancer disparities and integrative population data science. The investigator will advance causal inference and methodological rigor in observational cancer research, including approaches such as target trial emulation and methods addressing time varying exposures and confounding. Areas of interest include statistical epidemiology, cancer predisposition including obesity/alcoholism/aging structural and social determinants of cancer disparities and the integration of cohort, EHR, genomic, and neighborhood data. The successful candidate will strengthen research across the PS Program aims: cancer epidemiology and genomics, cancer prevention and early detection, and cancer outcomes."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/352": {
      "title": "Associate Professor - SOM Oncology Program",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: MD, United States The University of Maryland Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center (UMGCCC) is recruiting a full-time, tenure track Assistant or Associate Professor in Cancer Biostatistics. The University of Maryland Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center (UMGCCC) is recruiting a full-time, tenure track Assistant or Associate Professor in Cancer Biostatistics to join our thriving cancer research environment. This person will hold a primary appointment in the Division of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics in the Department of Epidemiology and Public Health at the University of Maryland School of Medicine. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent in Biostatistics or Statistics. The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent in Biostatistics, Statistics, Computer Science, or Applied Mathematics. Preference will be given to individuals who have demonstrated a record of supporting team science grants such as SPORE, P01, U01 and U54. The preferred candidate will also have established their own cutting-edge research and have shown the ability or the potential to attract external, peer-reviewed funding. The University of Maryland-Baltimore offers a rich research environment and the Greenbaum Comprehensive Cancer Center provides the opportunity and venue to impact cancer research through methodological innovation. An essential quality of the successful candidate is the ability to professionally and conscientiously support and manage complex data analysis projects in collaborative settings across teams of researchers and core services. Desired qualities include: 1) clinical trial expertise, 2) experience with collaborative grant writing and team science grants and 3) a record of cutting-edge research in biostatistics. In summary, the ideal candidate will be collaborative and have methodological versatility, with an interest in clinical trials and biostatistics as applied to cancer research. Qualified applicants should submit a single PDF file containing a short cover letter, a detailed curriculum vitae, contact information for three or more references, up to 3 reprints of representative publication(s), and a description of future research and scientific interests. Salary range: $125,00 to $200,000 annually The referenced salary range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. This salary range does not include all components of the Program in Oncology faculty compensation program or pay from participation in the Program in Oncology variable compensation programs. Therefore, the actual compensation paid to the selected candidate may vary from the salary range stated herein. For more information, please contact the hiring department. The referenced salary range represents the minimum and maximum salaries for this position and is based on the University of Maryland School of Medicine’s good faith belief at the time of posting. Not all candidates will be eligible for the upper end of the salary range. The actual compensation offered to the selected candidate may vary and will ultimately depend on multiple factors, which may include the successful candidate's geographic location, skills, work experience, internal equity, market conditions, education/training and other factors, as reasonably determined by the University. UMB offers a comprehensive benefits package that prioritizes wellness, work/life balance, and professional development. This position participates in a retirement program that must be selected and is effective on your date of hire. Faculty receive a generous leave package that includes over 4 weeks of vacation accrued each year, paid holidays, personal leave, unlimited accrual of sick time, and comprehensive health insurance; professional learning and development programs; tuition remission for employees and their dependents at any University System of Maryland school. The successful candidate will be a core faculty in the UMGCCC Biostatistics Shared Resource and will be required to support Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center investigators and the research infrastructure of the Center. Greenebaum Cancer Center includes 4 major research programs: Cancer Therapeutics, Population Science, Cancer Biology, and Tumor Immunology and Immunoengineering. The successful candidate will be a full-time member of the Biostatistics Shared Resource (BSR). The BSR is responsible for statistical collaboration across the Center, including the design and statistical oversight of clinical trials, and quantitative educational efforts related to the training of fellows and students. The Shared Resource currently includes 4 full-time PhD faculty from the Division of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics, as well as affiliated data science faculty from across the University of Maryland. There also is close collaboration with the Institute of Genome Sciences and the Institute for Health Computing."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/362": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow A - 10213000-SOM Institute For Genome Sciences",
      "description": "Category: Fellow JobFunction: Post Doctoral Fellow JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States Computational Postdoctoral Fellow The Computational Multiomics Laboratory at the University of Maryland School of Medicine (UMSOM) is recruiting computational postdoctoral fellow s to join our interdisciplinary team . The lab is led by Dr. Rongbin Zheng , who will join the University of Maryland School of Medicine in mid- December 2026 as a faculty member in the I nstitute for Genome Sciences (IGS) with affiliations to the Institute for Health Computing (IHC), the Program in Oncology , and the Department of Pharmacology and Physiology at UMSOM . Dr. Zheng is a K99/R00 Independence to Pathway Awardee and contributed to widely used genomics resources and studies published in Cell , Nature Metabolism , Nucleic Acids Research , and other leading journals. Research in the Zheng Lab focuses on decoding disease complexity through artificial intelligence (AI), multi-omics, and multicellular regulation to advance therapeutic discovery, with particular emphasis on genetic disorders, metabolic diseases, and cancer. Successful candidates will have opportunities to pursue independent research at the intersection of computational modeling, genetics, functional genomics, metabolism, cell signaling, and single-cell and spatial omics, with applications to understanding biological complexity in human diseases. The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. A PhD and/or MD in Data Science, Bioinformatics, Computational Biology, Biology, Statistics, Mathematics, Computer Science, or a related field. Strong interest in computational biology and interdisciplinary biomedical research. A strong publication track record. Excellent scientific communication skills, including oral presentations and scientific writing. Strong analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Proficiency in Python/R programming and Linux/Unix environments. What We Offer A collaborative, welcoming, and supportive research environment with interdisciplinary opportunities across IGS, IHC, and UMB. Individualized mentorship and career development support tailored to each researcher's professional goals. Opportunities to develop independent research directions and lead scientific projects. Opportunities to collaborate with researchers in computational biology, genomics, AI, and experimental biomedical sciences. Access to institutional research infrastructure, computational resources, and interdisciplinary scientific expertise. Competitive annual salary starting at $74,600, commensurate with experience and qualifications. We value scientific curiosity, creativity, collaboration, and intellectual independence. Our goal is to foster a supportive research environment where every team member can pursue exciting biological questions, develop innovative computational approaches, and grow toward their long-term career aspirations. How to Apply Please email the following materials to Rongbin ( RZheng@som.umaryland.edu ): An updated CV. Contact information for three professional references. A one-page research statement describing their research interests. Ideal candidates should have a strong background in computational biology and omics, with expertise or research interests in at least one of the following areas: Genetics, epigenetics, or functional genomics. Metabolism, metabolic diseases (particularly obesity and diabetes), or adipose biology. Cancer biology, tumor microenvironment, or immunometabolism. Artificial intelligence (AI), deep learning, or machine learning applied to biological questions. Single-cell and spatial omics."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/202": {
      "title": "Research Associate - SOM Oncology Program",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: Res Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: MD, United States The Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center and the Division of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics in the Department of Epidemiology and Public Health at the University of Maryland School of Medicine are recruiting a full-time, non-tenure track, faculty-level Research Associate with expertise in biostatistics to join a thriving cancer research environment. The Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center and the Division of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics in the Department of Epidemiology and Public Health at the University of Maryland School of Medicine are recruiting a full-time, non-tenure track, faculty-level Research Associate with expertise in biostatistics to join a thriving cancer research environment. The successful candidate will be committed to working with Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center members and will hold a primary appointment in the Department of Epidemiology and Public Health. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent in Biostatistics, Statistics, Computational Biology, or Mathematics. Primarily, this is a collaborative biostatistics position to help support the research infrastructure of the Cancer Center. This will involve working with a number of different cancer researchers on a range of projects, assisting with experimental design, data analyses, grant development, clinical trials, abstracts and manuscripts. Preference will be given to experienced individuals who have 1) demonstrated ability to professionally support and manage data analysis projects in collaborative settings across teams of researchers and core services, including grant writing; 2) demonstrated expertise in modern biostatistics, bioinformatics and/or AI methodologies, with cancer-related applications being especially of interest; 3) clinical trial protocol development expertise is a priority but it can be developed, as it will be a required aspect of the position; 4) a record of independent and innovative methodological research would be welcomed and supported as well. In sum, the ideal candidate will have methodological versatility across modern biostatistics and have a strong demonstrated interest and ability to work collaboratively. Qualifications - External Qualified applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent in Biostatistics, Statistics, Computer Science, or Applied Mathematics and should submit a single PDF file containing a short cover letter, a detailed curriculum vitae, contact information for three or more references, up to 3 reprints of representative publication(s), and a description of collaborative experience and future research interests The University of Maryland, Baltimore is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, color, religion, national origin, disability, protected Veteran status, age, or any other characteristic protected by law or policy. Minorities, women, protected veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. If accommodations are needed, contact Staffing & Career Services at 410-706-2606, Monday – Friday, 8:30am – 4:30pm EST. Maryland Relay can be accessed by dialing 711 (in-state) or 1-800-735-2258 The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. Doctorate degree in the field of specialization, or have relatively comparable experience. Capable of carrying out individual research or collaborating in group research at the advanced level; should be trained in research procedures; and should have had the experience and specialized training necessary to develop and interpret data required for success in such research projects as may be undertaken. May require unrestricted license in the State of Maryland. The appointee ordinarily should hold the doctoral degree in the field of specialization or have relatively comparable experience. The appointee should be capable of carrying out individual research or collaborating in group research at the advanced level; should be trained in research procedures; and should have had the experience and specialized training necessary to develop and interpret data required for success in such research projects as may be undertaken. This appointment is made annually, with reappointment possible for a maximum of six years."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/353": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Pharmacoeconomics), Tenure Track - School of Pharmacy",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States The University of Maryland School of Pharmacy is conducting an open search for an Assistant Professor, tenure track, in the Department of Practice, Sciences and Health Outcomes Research. This position requires teaching, scholarly activity including publications and serving as principal investigator on externally-funded grants, and service to the department, school, campus, professional societies, and the public. The position is a full-time, 12-month appointment based at our Baltimore campus. In addition to service and scholarly activity, this position will be responsible for serving as course manager for the Pharmacoeconomics & CER I course and teaching and/or serving as course manager for cost-effectiveness analysis, health economics and outcomes research in PharmD, MS and PhD courses as assigned by the department chair. The University of Maryland, Baltimore campus houses schools of Pharmacy, Medicine, Nursing, Social Work, Law, and Dentistry. The University of Maryland enjoys an excellent established record of accomplishment of successful collaboration for interprofessional practice and research collaborations with the other professional schools on campus. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS An earned PhD in Economics or equivalent degree with a strong focus on cost-effectiveness analysis and health economics and outcomes research. Excellent communication skills are essential. Evidence of scholarship is preferred. REQUIRED SKILLS, KNOWLEDGE, AND ABILITIES Professional conduct in all dealings with students, faculty, and administration. Communications – Knowledge of communication and dissemination techniques and methods, including alternative ways to inform via written, oral, and visual media. Initiative – Willing and able to take on responsibilities and challenges. Leadership – Willing and able to lead; take charge and offer opinions and direction. Learning Strategies – Selecting and using instructional methods and procedures appropriate in the discipline family when learning or teaching new things. Strong interpersonal abilities with excellent oral and written communication skills to effectively communicate with students, staff, faculty, and administration. Monitoring/assessing course content and research progress to make improvements or take corrective action. Stress Tolerance – Ability to accept criticism and deal calmly and effectively with high-stress situations. HOW TO APPLY Interested candidates must apply online through the Careers at UMB website and submit an initial application that includes a letter of intent (reference position number 05-180-102), curriculum vitae (signed and dated), statement of teaching philosophy, statement of scholarly interests, and names and contact information of at least three references. Shortlisted candidates will be required to submit three letters of recommendation from reviewers external to the University of Maryland School of Pharmacy. In addition to applying online, interested candidates may also send application materials to: C. Daniel Mullins, PhD Professor and Executive Director, the PATIENTS Program University of Maryland School of Pharmacy Department of Practice, Sciences, and Health Outcomes Research 220 Arch Street, 12th Floor Baltimore, MD 21201 Email: Daniel.Mullins@rx.umaryland.edu Phone: 410.706.0879 SALARY RANGE: Assistant Professor: $120,000 - $140,000 The salary will be commensurate with education, experience, and qualifications. Employees whose job duties require them to work in a clinical setting or at clinical or field sites continue to be subject to the vaccine requirements of those sites. For additional information on protocols and exemptions, please visit the COVID-19 website. UMB offers a comprehensive benefits package that prioritizes wellness, work/life balance, and professional development. This position participates in a retirement program that must be selected and is effective on your date of hire. Faculty receive a generous leave package that includes over four weeks of vacation accrued each year, paid holidays, personal leave, unlimited accrual of sick time, and comprehensive health insurance; professional learning and development programs; tuition remission for employees and their dependents at participating University System of Maryland schools. For more information, please visit HR Benefits at https://www.umaryland.edu/hrs/benefits-and-well-being/ For information regarding UMB Employee Perks, please visit https://www.umaryland.edu/hr/benefits-and-well-being/benefits-programs-and-perks/ UMB is committed to cultivating a diverse and inclusive workforce and is proud to be an equal employment opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, age, ancestry or national origin, sex, pregnancy or related conditions, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, physical or mental disability, marital status, protected veteran’s status, or any other legally protected classification. If you anticipate needing a reasonable accommodation for a disability under the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), during any part of the employment process, please submit a UMB Job Application Request . You may also contact HRDiversity@umaryland.edu. Please note that only inquiries concerning a request for a reasonable accommodation will be responded to from this email address. The University of Maryland, Baltimore prohibits sex discrimination in any education program or activity that it operates. Individuals may report concerns or questions to the Title IX Coordinator. For more information, follow this link UMB Notice of Non-Discrimination . SUPERVISORY CONTROLS This position is responsible to the Chair of the Department of Practice, Sciences, and Health Outcomes Research (P-SHOR). Teaching reporting responsibility is to the Chair of the Department through the Vice Chair for Academic Affairs. UMB CORE VALUES Respect & Integrity, Well-Being & Sustainability, Equity & Justice, and Innovation & Discovery ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS Lead cost-effectiveness and health economics and outcomes research instructional activities across academic programs at the school, supervise students; secure extramural funding; and actively support the vision and mission of the department, school and campus. SCHOLARSHIP Scholarly activity is required; publication and presentation of work is expected. Conduct original research in cost-effectiveness analysis, health economics and outcomes research, health technology assessment, and pharmaceutical health services research. Secure funding for research projects. Develop interdisciplinary research collaborations with colleagues within the School of Pharmacy as well as with colleagues in the other professional schools across campus. Publish research findings in high-impact peer-reviewed journals. Scholarly activity may be pursued either independently or in collaboration with others; however, serving as first author on manuscripts and principal investigator on funded grants is expected for successful progress towards promotion and tenure. SERVICE TO THE UNIVERSITY, SCHOOL, AND PUBLIC Participate in appropriate faculty activities (e.g., faculty assembly, department meetings, faculty committees) and other endeavors. Provide service to professional organizations, citizens of Maryland, and the public at large."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/236": {
      "title": "Faculty New Hire-Nursing Family & Community Health",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/11/2026 Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States The University of Maryland School of Nursing (UMSON) is currently recruiting for two full-time teaching-focused Assistant or Associate Professor to join the Community Public Specialty and the Department of Family and Community Health. The Community Public Health Specialty currently includes an MSN program with certificates in Environmental Health and Care Coordination and required community public health courses for BSN, MSNE, and DNP students. The ideal candidates are doctoral prepared nurses with an advanced degrees in community/public health and experience in community/public health/population health. The University of Maryland School of Nursing was founded in 1889 and is one of the premier nursing schools in the country. As a catalyst for creativity and collaboration, we engage diverse groups of professionals, organizations, and communities in addressing local, national, and global health priorities. Together, faculty, staff, and students create a rich and vibrant working and learning environment where knowledge is created and shared. Doctoral degree in nursing or related field required Leadership and management experience in community public health and population health Graduate level preparation in community health nursing or public health Prior academic experience required; teaching at the graduate level preferred Active nursing license or eligibility to practice nursing in Maryland or compact State required Familiarity with community/public health delivery sites in Maryland Hiring Range: $122,000-$148,000 Successful candidates will participate in the development, teaching, organization, and evaluation of clinical and didactic courses primarily in the graduate programs. In addition to teaching, responsibilities include advising graduate students; participating in the governance of the department/school; participating in research, scholarship; service, and other activities that advance the mission of the school."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/277": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow A - 10213000-SOM Institute For Genome Sciences",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/12/2026 Category: Fellow JobFunction: Post Doctoral Fellow JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States Post Doctoral Fellow – Translational Microbiome Research and Therapeutics POSTDOCTORAL POSITION IN TRANSLATIONAL MICROBIOME RESEARCH AND THERAPEUTICS AT THE UNIVERSITY OF MARYLAND SCHOOL OF MEDICINE, INSTITUTE FOR GENOME SCIENCES, CENTER FOR ADVANCED MICROBIOME RESEARCH & INNOVATION The research groups of Dr. Bing Ma at the Center for Advanced Microbiome Research & Innovation (CAMRI), Institute for Genome Sciences (IGS), Department of Microbiology and Immunology (MMI), and Dr. Jonathan Bromberg at the Department of Surgery and MMI, University of Maryland School of Medicine seek applicants for a postdoctoral scholar position. The fellow will be co-mentored by Drs. Ma and Bromberg and will work at the interface of microbiology, microbiome science, host–microbe interactions, immunology, and translational therapeutics . The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. Doctoral degree in Microbiology, Immunology, Molecular Biology, Genetics, Genomics, Bioinformatics, Computational Biology, Systems Biology, or a related field. Additional inquiries about the position can be sent to Drs. Bing Ma ( bma@som.umaryland.edu ) and Jonathan Bromberg ( jbromberg@som.umaryland.edu ). Interested applicants should submit a CV, a statement of research interests (2 pages maximum), and contact information for three references. Consideration of candidates will begin upon receipt of applications and will continue until the position is filled. Qualified candidates will be enthusiastic, highly motivated, and interested in understanding how gut microorganisms influence host physiology and disease and in developing microbiome-based therapeutic strategies. The research will focus on 1) how chronic immunosuppressive therapies reshape the gut microbiome and microbial metabolic functions; 2) how specific microorganisms and microbiome-derived metabolites interact with host immune and tissue responses to influence transplant outcomes; and 3) discovery, mechanistic characterization, and preclinical development of beneficial microbes, probiotics, defined microbial consortia, and postbiotics to restore host–microbiome homeostasis and improve clinical outcomes. A major emphasis of the position will be experimental and mechanistic microbiology. We have a strong preference for microbiologists with experience in microbial cultivation and working knowledge in computational analysis of omics data or, equally, computational biologists/statisticians with a deep appreciation of microbiology, microbial physiology, and biological mechanisms. Projects may involve isolation and cultivation of anaerobic gut microorganisms, strain-level phenotyping, microbial physiology and metabolism, host–microbe interaction studies, manipulation of microbial communities, and functional evaluation of candidate probiotic strains and microbial metabolites in cellular and animal models. The fellow will have opportunities to translate discoveries from human cohorts into experimentally testable microbial interventions and to investigate their mechanisms of action. The program combines microbial cultivation, molecular microbiology, immunology, metabolomics, and animal models with genomic and multi-omics technologies. Depending on the project, approaches may include microbial genome sequencing, metagenomics, metabolomics, transcriptomics, single-cell or spatial profiling, and computational analysis. These technologies will be used primarily to identify microbial functions, define mechanisms of probiotic or postbiotic activity, and guide development of microbiome-directed therapeutics. The ideal applicant is expected to: have experience with or a strong interest in anaerobic microbial cultivation, isolation and characterization of gut microorganisms, bacterial physiology, and experimental manipulation of microbial communities ; have a strong foundation in microbiology, microbial physiology, molecular biology, microbial ecology, or host–microbe interactions ; be interested in the development and functional characterization of probiotics, commensal microorganisms, microbial consortia, or microbiome-derived metabolites as therapeutic candidates; conduct mechanistic studies using microbial culture systems, molecular approaches, host-cell assays, and/or animal models; possess a working knowledge of genomic sciences, sequencing technologies, and microbiome and multi-omics analyses, including microbial genomics, metagenomics, metabolomics, and transcriptomics; experience with computational analysis of such data is advantageous but not required as the primary area of expertise ( training in relevant analytical approaches will be available); lead research projects from experimental design and microbial cultivation through functional validation, data interpretation, presentation, and publication; work effectively both independently and within a multidisciplinary team spanning microbiology, immunology, transplantation, genomics, and translational research. The successful candidate will join a highly interactive research environment with extensive expertise and infrastructure for microbiology, immunology, multi-omics, computational biology, and preclinical therapeutic development . The position provides a particular opportunity for a scientist interested in moving from fundamental microbial biology toward mechanism-based probiotic and microbiome therapeutics ."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/280": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow A - 10213000-SOM Institute For Genome Sciences",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/12/2026 Category: Fellow JobFunction: Post Doctoral Fellow JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States Postdoctoral Fellow - Epidemiology & Urogenital Microbiome University of Maryland School of Medicine Institute for Genome Sciences (IGS) Center for Advanced Microbiome Research and Innovation (CAMRI) The Brotman Laboratory is seeking a Postdoctoral Fellow in Epidemiology to join an NIH-funded study investigating the natural history of Mycoplasma genitalium (MG), an emerging and highly drug-resistant reproductive tract pathogen. Although MG is increasingly recognized as a cause of serious gynecologic disease, key questions about its natural history, persistence, and clinical outcomes in women remain unanswered. The fellow will leverage extensive omics data and specimens from the NIH Longitudinal Study of Vaginal Flora, a cohort of 3,620 women followed quarterly, with more than 13,000 archived cervicovaginal specimens and detailed longitudinal clinical data, including standardized symptom assessments and pelvic examinations. The project will examine: How often asymptomatic, untreated MG progresses to cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, and other clinical syndromes Whether MG is independently associated with these outcomes after accounting for co-infections How the vaginal microbiome and immune response influence progression to symptomatic disease versus spontaneous clearance This position offers substantial opportunities to lead first-author publications, present research at national and international scientific conferences, contribute to grant development, and build an independent research program at the intersection of epidemiology, infectious diseases, microbiome science, and women's health. The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. PhD, ScD, or equivalent doctoral degree in epidemiology, biostatistics, bioinformatics, microbiology, or a related field Strong quantitative and statistical skills, with proficiency in R or Python Experience analyzing longitudinal, infectious disease, clinical, or high-dimensional data Demonstrated record of peer-reviewed publications Strong scientific writing skills and the ability to translate complex analyses into clear scientific narratives Interest in microbiome science and women's health Experience with microbiome or multi-omic data analysis, causal inference methods, or infectious disease epidemiology is preferred but not required Research and Training Environment The fellow will join a collaborative, intellectually rigorous, and mission-driven research environment that brings together expertise in women’s health, epidemiology, biostatistics, genomics, bioinformatics, microbiology, and immunology. The position provides access to deeply characterized longitudinal cohorts, curated biospecimen repositories, and state-of-the-art genomic and computational infrastructure. Through mentorship, grant writing, and participation in future observational and interventional studies, the fellow will receive strong support in developing an independent research career. Application Interested applicants should submit: Curriculum vitae A brief statement of research interests and relevant experience Contact information for three references Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Conduct epidemiologic and statistical analyses of longitudinal clinical and high-dimensional microbiome data Apply and develop methods for multi-omic data integration, including metagenomic and immune-profiling data Interpret findings within epidemiologic and biological frameworks Lead first-author manuscripts and co-author publications in high-impact peer-reviewed journals Present research at national and international scientific meetings Collaborate closely with epidemiologists, statisticians, bioinformaticians, and laboratory scientists Contribute to study design, analytic plans, and grant development"
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/464": {
      "title": "Non-Adjunct Faculty - SOM Anesthesiology",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: Adjunct Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States Advance your career in the Maryland/DC region! We invite you to join our team of multispecialty anesthesiologists at the University of Maryland. We're seeking board-eligible/board-certified MD/DO anesthesiologists to support our rapidly expanding growth, working in operating room, procedural and obstetrics suite environments. Opportunities currently exist at our Baltimore Washington Medical Center (BWMC) practice. Duties include clinical care and some teaching of learners at various levels — student registered nurse anesthetists, residents, etc. BWMC has been recognized as one of the best hospitals in the region for more than 20 years. Our team is committed to the delivery of state-of-the-art anesthesia services and BWMC is home to leading-edge technology, nationally recognized quality, personalized service and outstanding people. Our academic department is dedicated to helping faculty obtain outstanding skills and additional certifications in ultrasound, simulation, quality/safety/clinical research and expertise in many other disciplines. Our department has a very strong research profile (#14 national 2024 Blue Ridge Ranking) and a major focus on quality/safety/clinical research, supported by a perioperative data warehouse and membership in the Multicenter Perioperative Outcomes Group (MPOG). We also offer excellent benefits (including tuition remission for both faculty and dependents), loan forgiveness, competitive compensation and ample opportunities for growth and advancement. Expected rank for this position will be Assistant Professor or higher, however, final rank and tenure status will be commensurate with selected candidate's experience. The referenced salary range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. This salary range does not include all components of the Department of Anesthesiology faculty compensation program or pay from participation in Department of Anesthesiology incentive compensation programs. Therefore, the actual compensation paid to the selected candidate may vary from the salary range stated herein. For more information, please contact the hiring department. The referenced salary range represents the minimum and maximum salaries for this position and is based on the University of Maryland School of Medicine’s good faith belief at the time of posting. Not all candidates will be eligible for the upper end of the salary range. The actual compensation offered to the selected candidate may vary and will ultimately depend on multiple factors, which may include the successful candidate's geographic location, skills, work experience, internal equity, market conditions, education/training and other factors, as reasonably determined by the University. UMB offers a comprehensive benefits package that prioritizes wellness, work/life balance, and professional development. This position participates in a retirement program that must be selected and is effective on your date of hire. Faculty receive a generous leave package that includes over 4 weeks of vacation accrued each year, paid holidays, personal leave, unlimited accrual of sick time, and comprehensive health insurance; professional learning and development programs; tuition remission for employees and their dependents at any University System of Maryland school. For immediate consideration, please submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae and contact information of three references via this online application. For additional questions after application, please contact: David Mintz, MD, PhD, Chairman, Department of Anesthesiology, University of Maryland School of Medicine, 22 South Greene Street, S11C00 Baltimore, MD 21201 Office Phone: 410-328-9837 dmintz@som.umaryland.edu Must be board-eligible or board-certified. Doctorate or recognized terminal degree in the field of specialization. Demonstrated a degree of excellence experience in teaching, clinical or professional practice in a field of specialization establish an outstanding regional and national reputation among colleagues. Appointees shall also have demonstrated extraordinary scholarly competence and leadership in the profession. Unrestricted license in the State of Maryland Faculty members at the University who are: Appointed to provide contractually defined responsibilities such as research, service, or instruction for non-credit bearing courses; Neither tenured nor eligible for tenure;"
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/518": {
      "title": "Research Associate - SOM IHV Epidemiology Division",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/11/2026 Category: Faculty JobFunction: Res Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States The Institute of Human Virology at the University of Maryland School of Medicine is recruiting a full-time Faculty Research Associate in the Department of Epidemiology. The primary goal of this position is to advance implementation of science and health services research at the University of Maryland, Baltimore. The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. PhD in public health, implementation science, health services research, epidemiology, sociology, anthropology, psychology, nursing, or a health related field. Postdoctoral training in implementation science, mixed methods research, health services research, HIV, infectious diseases, or a related discipline is required. Demonstrated experience in mixed methods research, including qualitative and quantitative study design, data collection, and analysis. Experience in implementation research, program evaluation, and stakeholder-engaged research. Experience designing and conducting impact and evaluation studies. Familiarity with U.S. Government agencies, NIH, CDC, international donors, and donor reporting requirements. Strong qualitative and quantitative data management and analytic skills. Experience developing manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals. Experience contributing to grant applications and funded research projects. Experience teaching in an academic setting and mentoring students or trainees. Experience using qualitative and quantitative software platforms, including MAXQDA, NVivo, ATLAS.ti, R, SAS, Stata, or equivalent software. Excellent written and oral communication skills in English. Ability to communicate and coordinate effectively with multidisciplinary teams, stakeholders, and project partners. Ability to train and develop capacity in data collection, analysis, and data utilization. Experience in HIV, infectious diseases, health equity, healthcare delivery, or global health research is required. To apply for this position, please send a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, and contact information for 3 references to: pvillalba@ihv.umaryland.edu . The University of Maryland School of Medicine offers a competitive salary and benefits. Appointments are made commensurate with education and experience. The University of Maryland, Baltimore is an Equal Opportunity, Affirmative Action Employer. Minorities, women, veterans and individuals with disabilities are encouraged to apply. Undertake advanced qualitative, quantitative, and mixed methods analyses of research and program data to address research questions and inform healthcare delivery and implementation strategies. Collaborate with multidisciplinary teams to develop, manage, and analyze datasets derived from electronic health records, surveys, interviews, focus groups, programmatic data, and other sources. Support Principal Investigators in the design, implementation, and evaluation of research studies, implementation initiatives, and program evaluations. Contribute to the development of research protocols, data collection instruments, and NIH and other research funding applications. Build research and scientific writing capacity among internal and external collaborators through mentorship of students, trainees, research staff, and community partners. Support study design, implementation, and monitoring activities, including the development and review of data collection tools, oversight of field activities, qualitative and quantitative data collection, data analysis, interpretation, and dissemination of findings. Lead the design and conduct of implementation science, health services research, and mixed methods evaluations and contribute to methodological innovation in implementation research. Apply implementation science frameworks and theories to evaluate implementation determinants, strategies, fidelity, reach, adoption, acceptability, appropriateness, feasibility, penetration, and sustainability. Actively contribute to peer-reviewed publications, scientific presentations, technical reports, and dissemination products. Contribute to proposal development by assisting with study design, implementation plans, evaluation frameworks, outcome measures, and research methodology sections. Collaborate with multidisciplinary teams to prepare manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals and develop products for scientific, clinical, and community audiences. Participate in teaching, mentoring, and training activities related to implementation science, mixed methods research, epidemiology, and public health."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/537": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor -SOM Child Psychiatry General",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States The University of Maryland School of Medicine, Division of Child and Adolescent Psychiatry is seeking a full-time child and adolescent psychiatrist and psychologist. The positions carry faculty appointments at the University and offer exciting opportunities for clinical care, teaching and research. Academic rank and salary are commensurate with experience. Expected rank is Assistant Professor or higher, however, rank and tenure status is dependent on candidate’s qualifications. Please attach your letter of introduction and CV. Salary range for Assistant Professor MD, Part-time 50% FTE: $116,00-$170,000 Salary range for Assistant Professor MD, full-time 100% FTE: $230,000-$330,000 The referenced salary range reflects base pay for Assistant Professor which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. This salary range does not include all components of the Department faculty compensation program or pay from participation in department variable compensation programs. Therefore the actual compensation paid to the selected candidate may vary from the salary range stated herein. For more information, please contact the hiring department. Doctorate or recognized terminal degree in the field of specialization. Shown potential for superior teaching, service, and research, scholarship, or r creative performance, commensurate with the mission of the UMB. May require unrestricted license in the State of Maryland. Clinical responsibilities will include time working in our Ambulatory Child Psychiatry Clinics, Department of Juvenile Services, and Maryland Medicaid Peer Review Antipsychotic Monitoring Program. You will be providing direct clinical care in the outpatient programs including medication management and therapeutic interactions with children and families, collaborating with the multidisciplinary team, and working with families and school personnel and others involved in the patients’ lives. As a member of the faculty, you will be part of the weekend attending team and be assigned rounds between 4-7 times per year, including approximately 1 holiday every year and 1-3 months per year of supervision to fellows covering consultation to pediatric and shock trauma floors. As a faculty appointee you will play an active role in the educational and training programs in the department as well as having responsibilities in the areas of teaching and departmental committee work. You will also be providing supervision of fellows, residents, medical students, and other trainees. The appointee should also show potential for superior teaching, service, and research, scholarship, or creative performance, commensurate with the mission of the institution."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/539": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - SOM Psych CSMHA School",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States The University of Maryland School of Medicine, Division of Child and Adolescent Psychiatry is seeking a full-time child and adolescent psychologist researcher to work with the National Center School Mental Health. Preference will be given to candidates with specialization and expertise in school mental health services, assessment, and policy. This position offers an exceptional opportunity to engage in research to strengthen policies andprograms in school mental health to improve learning and promote success for America’syouth. The faculty will be expected to work on projects that aim to implement and scale school and community-based mental health programs to improve the quality of life of children and adolescents with complex health care needs. This work will facilitate the advancement of mental health prevention and intervention programs for student populations in school and community settings, specifically students with developmental and physical disabilities, chronic illness, complex health needs and other students The positions carry faculty appointments at the University and offer exciting opportunities forclinical care, teaching and research. Academic rank and salary are commensurate withexperience. Expected rank is Assistant Professor or higher, however, rank and tenure status is dependent on candidate’s qualifications. Doctorate or recognized terminal degree in the field of specialization. Shown potential for superior teaching, service, and research, scholarship, or r creative performance, commensurate with the mission of the UMB. May require unrestricted license in the State of Maryland. Engaging in research projects that aim to implement and scale school and community-based mental health programs to improve the quality of life of children and adolescents with special education and complex health care needs. This work will facilitate the advancement of mental health prevention and intervention programs for student populations in school and community settings, specifically students in special education programs with developmental and physical disabilities, chronic illness, complex health needs and other marginalized students. Specific research and implementation strategies include monitoring and evaluation efforts to ensure school-based mental health programs are addressing the unique needs of special education students, adapting trauma, mental health, and SEL screening tools to meet the developmental needs of all students; providing training for peer-to-peer mental health support and; incorporating knowledge and skills of evidence-based mental health practices in teacher education curricula. This research will help increase the access to and quality of mental health programs in schools and specifically for students with special education and complex health care needs. The appointee should also show potential for superior teaching, service, and research, scholarship, or creative performance, commensurate with the mission of the institution."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/546": {
      "title": "Faculty New Hire - SOM Institute for Health Computing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/11/2026 Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: United States The University of Maryland Institute for Health Computing in North Bethesda, Maryland, seeks an accomplished scholar and thought leader in computer science and artificial intelligence to join our faculty as an Assistant or Associate Professor, commensurate with experience. The successful candidate will be based at the University of Maryland Institute for Health Computing (UM-IHC), a major computational research hub in North Bethesda that applies next-generation computational analytics and artificial intelligence to improve health and human disease. The UM-IHC is equipped with a high-performance computing cluster and access to multiple real world clinical data sets. While the position sits within a health and biomedical research environment, its scope extends broadly across computer science and AI — including machine learning, deep learning, natural language processing, and emerging foundation model architectures — with the expectation that the successful candidate will help set research direction and computational strategy for the Institute, not simply apply existing methods to clinical problems. The ideal candidate will bring an independent, extramurally funded research program, a strong publication record, and a vision for how advances in computer science and AI can transform biomedical and clinical research. The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. Required Qualifications: Strong programming skills (e.g., Python, R, or comparable languages) and experience building and deploying computational pipelines at scale. Demonstrated expertise in machine learning and/or artificial intelligence, with strong foundations in computer science (algorithms, systems, or theory) alongside applied statistical methods. Experience applying AI/ML methods to clinical, biomedical, or health-related data. Experience with clinical data and clinical research methods. A record of independent, peer-reviewed scholarship and grant-seeking appropriate to the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. Excellent analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to lead and collaborate across disciplinary teams. Excellent written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills. Experience mentoring or supervising students, postdoctoral fellows, or research staff. Preferred Qualifications: Expertise in deep learning frameworks (e.g., PyTorch , TensorFlow) and modern architectures such as transformers, foundation models, or large language models. Experience building or leading research teams or labs, with a demonstrated commitment to health equity and responsible AI. A significant, sustained publication record in top computer science/AI venues (e.g., NeurIPS , ICML, ACL) and/or health informatics journals. A track record of funded grants as principal investigator or co-investigator. Required Education and Experience: Doctoral degree in computer science, artificial intelligence, statistics, data science, or a closely related field. Eligible for faculty appointment at an academic medical center (or equivalent research institution) at the rank of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor; final rank will be determined by research record, publication history, and funding trajectory. For appointment at the rank of Associate Professor: a sustained, nationally recognized record of independent research, extramural funding, and scholarly leadership in computer science or artificial intelligence. For appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor: an emerging independent research trajectory, with strong doctoral or postdoctoral research training in computer science or artificial intelligence. Provide strategic and technical thought leadership on computational methods and AI strategy for the Institute for Health Computing. Integrate advanced mathematical and computational modeling to predict health outcomes such as treatment response, hospitalizations , and other clinically meaningful endpoints. Establish and lead an independent, extramurally funded research program in computer science and artificial intelligence, with applications spanning clinical, biological, environmental, and other complex health-related data domains. Secure and sustain extramural research funding. Design and oversee data analysis and computational pipelines, including data extraction, cleaning, transformation, quality assurance, and pipeline monitoring and maintenance. Develop novel machine learning, deep learning, and artificial intelligence methodologies — including next-generation approaches such as foundation models and large language models — and apply them to health and biomedical big data. Publish high-impact, peer-reviewed research in leading computer science, artificial intelligence, biomedical informatics, and clinical journals. Design and conduct research studies in collaboration with other faculty, students, postdoctoral fellows, and research staff. Collaborate with clinicians, scientists, and cross-disciplinary teams to translate computational and AI research findings into clinical and biomedical practice . Promote responsible, equitable AI by developing methods that identify and mitigate bias and advance health equity. Mentor graduate students, postdoctoral fellows, and junior researchers, and represent the Institute in the broader computer science/AI research community (e.g., conference leadership, editorial boards, advisory panels)."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/268": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - SOM Trauma Plastic Surgery",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States The Division of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery at the University of Maryland invites applications for a full-time academic plastic surgeon at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. Based primarily at the internationally acclaimed R Adams Cowley Shock Trauma Center, this position offers an exceptional opportunity to develop an academic career in craniofacial trauma and reconstruction, surgical education, and clinical research at one of the nation's busiest Level I trauma centers. The Division of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery at the University of Maryland invites applications for a full-time academic plastic surgeon at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. Based primarily at the internationally acclaimed R Adams Cowley Shock Trauma Center, this position offers an exceptional opportunity to develop an academic career in craniofacial trauma and reconstruction, surgical education, and clinical research at one of the nation's busiest Level I trauma centers. Our faculty provide comprehensive care for patients with facial fractures, complex craniofacial injuries, facial soft tissue trauma, and post-traumatic craniofacial deformities. The practice spans the full continuum of care—from acute injury management through secondary and tertiary reconstruction—offering a unique combination of high-volume craniofacial trauma, complex craniofacial reconstruction, and longitudinal patient care. The breadth and volume of the practice provide an excellent environment for developing expertise in craniofacial trauma while building a nationally recognized academic career. We seek an academically driven plastic surgeon with outstanding clinical judgment, technical excellence and a collaborative approach to multidisciplinary trauma care. The successful candidate will be committed to advancing our mission through patient-centered care, education, innovation, and scholarly activity. Qualified applicants must have completed an ACGME-accredited residency in Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery (BC/BE) and be eligible for unrestricted medical licensure in Maryland. Fellowship training in craniofacial surgery or significant experience in post-traumatic reconstruction is preferred but not required. In addition to the craniofacial trauma practice, faculty are encouraged and supported to develop an elective subspecialty practice aligned with their expertise and academic interests such as secondary craniofacial reconstruction, orthognathic surgery, facial reanimation, oculoplastic surgery, facial aesthetic surgery, or other areas that complement our mission. The successful candidate will play a key role in the training of residents through the combined University of Maryland/Johns Hopkins Plastic Surgery Residency Program, with opportunities for mentorship, curriculum development, and medical student education. The Division fosters academic careers through multidisciplinary collaboration, investigator-initiated clinical and translational research, mentorship, and protected opportunities for scholarly development."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/451": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Fellow B - SOM MPRC Administration",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/11/2026 Category: Fellow JobFunction: Post Doctoral Fellow JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States Advance your research career with an expert team, innovative tools, and opportunities to publish, write grants, and mentor others. Developing new statistical methods to model multimodal neuroimaging data, multi-omics data, electronic health record (EHR) and genetics data which are measured by longitudinally and cross-sectionally. Developing and applying machine learning and AI approaches to identify interactive topological relationships between various imaging modalities and multi-omics during aging and development. Implementing computationally intensive algorithms on high-performance computational clusters . Developing AI/ML-based causal inference models for real-world evidence applications (e.g., target trial emulations- TTE) Developing data integration methods to combine multiple massive datasets (UKBB, HCP, ENIGMA, and etc) and local trials and observational studies (e.g, UMMS EHR). Manuscript drafting and preparation. Performing other duties as assigned Doctorate or recognized terminal degree in the field of specialization. Demonstrated research abilities. May require unrestricted license in the State of Maryland. Post Doctoral Fellow."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/494": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor-SOM Dermatology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/11/2027 Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States The Department of Dermatology at the University of Maryland School of Medicine seeks to recruit a candidate for a full­time position in general medical dermatology. The Department of Dermatology at the University of Maryland School of Medicine seeks to recruit a candidate for a full­time position in general medical dermatology. The successful candidate will join our Department of Dermatology at the University of Maryland School of Medicine and provide patient care and education within our residency training program. The ideal candidate will have a strong interest, both in practicing and teaching general dermatology, as well as non-Mohs surgical dermatology. Particular areas of subspecialty interest might include inpatient consultative dermatology, complex medical dermatology, and/or dermatology of skin of color. A commitment to resident education is necessary. Expected rank is Assistant Professor/Associate Professor, however, rank and tenure status is dependent upon candidate's qualifications. Qualified Candidate will have a strong interest in practicing clinical dermatology and be Board certified or eligible. Expected salary range for Assistant Professors is $284,466 (min) and $569,296 (max). The referenced salary range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. This salary range does not include all components of the Dermatology faculty compensation program or pay from participation in Dermatology variable compensation programs. Therefore, the actual compensation paid to the selected candidate may vary from the salary range stated herein. For more information, please contact the hiring department. The referenced salary range represents the minimum (25%) and maximum (90%) salaries for this position and is based on the University of Maryland School of Medicine's good faith belief 