    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/489": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Fellow B - Dentistry Microbial IMET (843061-2)",
      "description": "Category: Fellow JobFunction: Post Doctoral Fellow JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States NIH-Funded Research Opportunities in the Chatterjee Laboratory The Chatterjee Laboratory at the University of Maryland, Baltimore is seeking two Postdoctoral Fellows to join an NIH-sponsored research program investigating the mechanistic basis of antibiotic resistance in Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) and its role in bacterial pathogenesis. These positions offer opportunities to address fundamental and clinically important questions in bacterial pathogenesis and antimicrobial resistance while collaborating with leading investigators in the field. Successful candidates will develop independent research projects commensurate with their experience and expertise, with the expectation that these projects will lead to peer-reviewed publications. The University of Maryland School of Dentistry (UMSOD) is the world's first dental college and Maryland’s predominant provider of comprehensive and emergency oral health services for individuals of all ages, including the underserved and disadvantaged. Through research innovation, creativity, and productive partnerships, our faculty, staff, and students are developing and implementing novel approaches to advance oral health and improve lives. Doctoral degree in Microbiology, Molecular Biology, Biochemistry, Biomedical Sciences, or a related field. Demonstrated success in conducting mechanistic research in bacterial pathogenesis. Experience working with Staphylococcus aureus or related Gram-positive bacteria is desirable. Ability to work independently, interpret datasets, and think critically. Strong collaborative skills and willingness to work within multidisciplinary teams. Opportunities Lead independent research projects. Publish in high-impact journals. Establish collaborations with investigators within and outside the institution. Present research findings at national and international conferences. Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover letter describing current and previous research experience, career goals, and research interests Contact information for three professional references Hiring range: $63,480 - $63,900 per year (depending on academic rank and commensurate with education and experience). Research Focus The laboratory seeks to understand: Molecular mechanisms underlying antibiotic resistance in MRSA The relationship between antibiotic resistance and bacterial pathogenesis Novel determinants of host-pathogen interactions Mechanisms that contribute to bacterial adaptation and survival"
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/493": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor -SOM Family Medicine",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States Assistant/Associate Professor: The Department of Family and Community Medicine at the University of Maryland School of Medicine is seeking a full-time Clinician-Educator to join our growing academic primary care team. This is an exciting opportunity for an innovative educator who is passionate about primary care, advancing medical education, and inspiring the next generation of family physicians. As the School of Medicine expands its medical student class size, the successful candidate will play a key leadership role in developing educational programs and curricula, recruiting and supporting community-based primary care preceptors, and creating meaningful learning experiences for medical students and residents. Assistant/Associate Professor : The Department of Family and Community Medicine at the University of Maryland School of Medicine is seeking a full-time Clinician-Educator to join our growing academic primary care team. This is an exciting opportunity for an innovative educator who is passionate about primary care, advancing medical education, and inspiring the next generation of family physicians. As the School of Medicine expands its medical student class size, the successful candidate will play a key leadership role in developing educational programs and curricula, recruiting and supporting community-based primary care preceptors, and creating meaningful learning experiences for medical students and residents. The position offers opportunities to contribute to medical education scholarship, foster innovation in teaching, and advance initiatives that encourage more students to pursue careers in Family Medicine, while providing comprehensive patient care at our University Family Medicine practice in Baltimore. The ideal candidate will be committed to serving the community and advancing the clinical, educational, and academic missions of the Department and the School of Medicine. This position includes serving as: Assistant Clerkship Director for the Family Medicine Clerkship Physician Director of Preceptor Recruitment and Retention Transition to Residency Course Director Primary Responsibilities Clinical Faculty Maintain an active outpatient clinical practice providing comprehensive family medicine and may include occasional on-call coverage and home calls. Teach and supervise medical students and residents in the clinical setting. Participate in scholarly activity, departmental conferences, committees, and academic initiatives. Assistant Clerkship Director Partner with the Clerkship Director to oversee the Family Medicine clerkship. Mentor and advise medical students, including those pursuing careers in Family Medicine. Participate in medical school admission interviews. Conduct curriculum development, student assessment, grading, clerkship quality improvement, and clinical site development. Collaborate with student organizations and departmental educational committees. Director of Preceptor Recruitment & Retention Lead statewide physician engagement efforts to recruit, develop, and retain community-based clinical preceptors. Cultivate relationships with physician practices, Federally Qualified Health Centers, community health centers, and regional Area Health Education Centers partners. Conduct site visits, support preceptor orientation and training, and enhance the quality of community-based medical education. Guide statewide initiatives focused on expanding clinical training opportunities in rural and underserved communities. Transition to Residency Course Director Direct the Department's Transition to Residency course for graduating medical students entering Family Medicine residency. Strengthen curriculum focused on clinical readiness, procedural skills, practice management, and professional development. Coordinate faculty, course logistics, student assessment, and continuous program improvement. Expected rank for this position will be Assistant Professor or higher, however, final rank and tenure status will be commensurate with selected candidate’s experience. Expected salary range is $209,000 (min) and $317,051 (max). The referenced salary range reflects base pay, which is based on faculty rank and years in rank. This salary range does not include all components of the Family Medicine faculty compensation program or pay from participation in Family Medicine’s variable compensation program. Therefore, the actual compensation paid to the selected candidate may vary from the salary range stated herein. For more information, please contact the hiring department. The University of Maryland School of Medicine (SOM) was chartered in 1807 as the first public medical school in the United States. It continues today as one of the fastest growing, top-tier biomedical research enterprises in the world. SOM is dedicated to providing excellence in biomedical education, basic and clinical research, quality patient care and service to improve the health of the citizens of Maryland and beyond. Candidates must be board certified/eligible in Family Medicine and eligible for unrestricted license in the State of Maryland. This position requires a medical degree from a recognized accredited domestic university (or foreign equivalent), strong commitment to patient care and teaching, and the ability to work well in a team setting. Expected rank for this position will be Assistant Professor or higher, however, final rank and tenure status will be commensurate with selected candidate’s experience. The Maryland Rural Health Transformation Program is supported by the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) of the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) as part of a financial assistance award to the Maryland Department of Health totaling $168,180,837.61 with 100 percent funded by CMS/HHS. The contents of this meeting are those of the author(s) and do not necessarily represent the official views of, nor an endorsement, by CMS/HHS, or the U.S. Government. Clinical Faculty Maintain an active outpatient clinical practice providing comprehensive family medicine and may include occasional on-call coverage and home calls. Teach and supervise medical students and residents in the clinical setting. Participate in scholarly activity, departmental conferences, committees, and academic initiatives. Assistant Clerkship Director Partner with the Clerkship Director to oversee the Family Medicine clerkship. Mentor and advise medical students, including those pursuing careers in Family Medicine. Participate in medical school admission interviews. Conduct curriculum development, student assessment, grading, clerkship quality improvement, and clinical site development. Collaborate with student organizations and departmental educational committees. Director of Preceptor Recruitment & Retention Lead statewide physician engagement efforts to recruit, develop, and retain community-based clinical preceptors. Cultivate relationships with physician practices, Federally Qualified Health Centers, community health centers, and regional Area Health Education Centers partners. Conduct site visits, support preceptor orientation and training, and enhance the quality of community-based medical education. Guide statewide initiatives focused on expanding clinical training opportunities in rural and underserved communities. Transition to Residency Course Director Direct the Department's Transition to Residency course for graduating medical students entering Family Medicine residency. Strengthen curriculum focused on clinical readiness, procedural skills, practice management, and professional development. Coordinate faculty, course logistics, student assessment, and continuous program improvement."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/425": {
      "title": "Clinical Instructor PT- SOD AOST Endodontics (.20 FTE)",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: Clin Faculty JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States The University of Maryland School of Dentistry (UMSOD) seeks a mission-critical Clinical Instructor in Endodontics to support excellence in specialty education and patient care within our Advanced Dental Education Program. This part-time (.2 FTE) position plays a critical role in maintaining the strength of both our postgraduate and predoctoral endodontic programs. The University of Maryland School of Dentistry (UMSOD) is the world's first dental college and Maryland’s predominant provider of comprehensive and emergency oral health services for individuals of all ages, including the underserved and disadvantaged. Through research innovation, creativity, and productive partnerships, our faculty, staff, and students are developing and implementing novel approaches to advance oral health and improve lives. Required: DDS/DMD or equivalent degree Completion of an ADA-accredited Endodontics residency program Board Certified by the American Board of Endodontics or actively working toward board certification Eligible for dental licensure in the State of Maryland Preferred: Demonstrated excellence in clinical endodontics Experience in postgraduate and predoctoral teaching, including simulation laboratory instruction Strong mentorship, communication, and organizational skills Commitment to specialty education Hiring range: $27,000 - $31,000 per year (commensurate with education and experience). Please submit the following, a signed/dated detailed curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching interests, and three letters of recommendation into the portal. Primary Responsibilities Clinical Teaching Provide direct supervision and instruction in the Postgraduate Endodontics Program. Mentor residents in diagnosis, treatment planning, case execution, microsurgical techniques, and evidence-based clinical decision-making. Supervise and instruct predoctoral students in preclinical laboratory exercises and clinical endodontic care. Ensure delivery of high-quality, ethical, and patient-centered specialty care. Participate in resident/student assessment, competency evaluations, and calibration activities. Foster a professional, collaborative, and accountable clinical learning environment. Didactic Teaching Contribute to postgraduate seminars, case conferences, literature review sessions, and board-style discussions. Deliver lectures in predoctoral endodontic didactic courses. Assist in curriculum development and integration of contemporary evidence-based endodontics. Support assessment of student and resident learning outcomes."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/428": {
      "title": "Open Rank Clinical Instructor or Clinical Assistant Professor - SOD General Practice",
      "description": "Category: Faculty JobFunction: General Faculty JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States The University of Maryland, Baltimore, School of Dentistry is looking for a full-time non-tenure clinical faculty member to join the Department of Comprehensive Dentistry. We are looking for someone who genuinely enjoys teaching, takes pride in mentoring the next generation of dentists, and wants to grow in academic dentistry over the long term. This role sits at the intersection of clinical education, patient care, and day-to-day academic operations. You will be directly shaping how pre-doctoral students develop into confident, competent dentists. This position is based in the Division of General Practice within the Department of Comprehensive Dentistry. The Division operates on a Group Practice model, organized into eight group practices. The 40 hours weekly will be distributed according to the scheduled clinic hours: Monday-Friday, 8am-5pm, including evening clinics from 4:00- 6:30 pm. The University of Maryland School of Dentistry (UMSOD) is the world's first dental college and Maryland’s predominant provider of comprehensive and emergency oral health services for individuals of all ages, including the underserved and disadvantaged. Through research innovation, creativity, and productive partnerships, our faculty, staff, and students are developing and implementing novel approaches to advance oral health and improve lives. DMD or DDS from a CODA-accredited institution, or a foreign equivalent dental degree Eligibility for a teaching dental license in Maryland The applicant must have a minimum of 5-years of clinical practice in a comprehensive general dentistry setting. Solid hands-on experience in clinical experience in comprehensive patient care, including phased treatment planning, operative and direct restorative dentistry, single-unit indirect restorations, urgent dental care, post-operative management, and generating appropriate referrals Working proficiency with Microsoft Office tools, including Word, Excel, and PowerPoint Please submit the following, a signed/dated detailed curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching interests, and three letters of recommendation into the portal. Hiring range: $135,000 to $150,000 (depending on academic rank and commensurate with education and experience). Day to day, your responsibilities will include: Teaching in the predoctoral dental clinic, supervising students providing patient care. Mentoring assigned students on academic and clinical progress throughout the clinical curriculum. Managing patient care with students within their assigned group practice. Track student progress and collaborate with academic affairs on student deficiencies. Teach and direct appropriate predoctoral courses tied to the department, including treatment planning, restorative dentistry, and diagnostic sciences. Assist the staff, faculty, and administration in training adjunct faculty to achieve clinical calibration. Supporting quality assurance, compliance, and improvement efforts in collaboration with the Division Director and the Associate Dean for Clinical Affairs. Responding to operational needs and special assignments from the Chair of the Department of Comprehensive Dentistry. Contributing as a genuine team member in identifying problems and working through solutions."
    },
    "https://efzv.fa.us6.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1013/job/431": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Pediatric Surgery",
      "description": "Category: Healthcare JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Baltimore, MD, United States FACULTY POSITION DEPARTMENT OF SURGERY, DIVISION OF PEDIATRIC SURGERY The University of Maryland seeks candidates to join the full-time faculty of the Division of Pediatric Surgery at the rank of Assistant Professor. Candidates should be board certified in Pediatric Surgery or currently in training in an accredited Pediatric Surgery fellowship program. Responsibilities will include clinical care of pediatric surgery patients, education of students, residents, and pediatric surgery fellows. Candidates should have a clear plan for academic development and scholarly activity that is consistent with the mission of the University of Maryland School of Medicine. Opportunities for basic and clinical research are available for appropriate and interested candidates. The School of Medicine is located in downtown Baltimore, Maryland, near the famous Inner Harbor and Camden Yards. The University of Maryland School of Medicine offers a full package of benefits and is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Interested individuals should apply and send an updated curriculum vitae and a brief statement of academic and clinical interests to: Kimberly Lumpkins, MD, MBA, FACS, FRCS Vice Chair, Strategy and Operations Department of Surgery Chief, Division of Pediatric Surgery 29 South Greene Street, Suite 110 University of Maryland School of Medicine Baltimore, MD, 21201 KLumpkins@som.umaryland.edu UMB is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, color, religion, national origin, disability, protected Veteran status, age, or any other characteristic protected by law or policy. We value diversity and how it enriches our academic and scientific community and strive toward cultivating an inclusive environment that supports all employees. Expected rank is Assistant Professor or higher, however, rank and tenure status is dependent on candidate’s qualifications. Further information about the Department of Surgery and UMSOM can be obtained at the following websites: http://medschool.umaryland.edu/ Qualifications Candidates should be board certified or eligible in pediatric surgery or currently in training in an ACGME accredited pediatric surgery fellowship program. Qualifications Candidates should be board certified or eligible in pediatric surgery or currently in training in an ACGME accredited pediatric surgery fellowship program. "
